    "background_raw": "https://shared.akamai.steamstatic.com/store_item_assets/steam/apps/5074510/39af146d72b16a1cbc8ed3ea51eb9053c761d975/page_bg_raw.jpg",
    "content_descriptors": {
      "ids": [
        5
      ],
      "notes": "Hide & Shriek is a horror game about hiding from a seeker in a derelict asylum. Its mature content is violence, and the violence is stylised and distant: characters are low poly models in a deliberately coarse late nineties console style, drawn small under a fixed three quarter overhead camera, and a hider the seeker catches is struck at melee range, leaves a short trail of red pixels on the floor and collapses into a body that another hider can revive. There is no weapon detail, no gore, no dismemberment and no close up death animation. The horror is atmosphere and tension rather than imagery, built out of darkness, footsteps, the edge of a torch beam and the pressure of being hunted, with startle moments when the seeker finds you.\r\n\r\nThe asylum is a setting rather than a subject. It is an abandoned building the players hide in, with rooms including a ward and a surgery, and the game contains no depiction of mental illness, of patients or of treatment, and tells no story about them.\r\n\r\nThe shop items are British domestic comedy, so alcohol and vaping appear as joke objects with names like Nan's sherry, the novelty beer helmet and the emotional support vape. Nothing is consumed on screen and none of it produces intoxication in game. Humour of the same register includes mild toilet jokes.\r\n\r\nThe game contains no sexual content, nudity, sexual assault or non consensual content, no depiction of suicide, self harm or substance abuse, and no real world hate content or political themes.\r\n\r\nOnline play includes voice chat between players over an in game radio, so players may hear speech from other players that is not created or moderated by the developer."
    }
  },
